<p>I've been sort of half-assing my Linux backups for a while now. I finally decided to fix them.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p>My backups were a combination of rsync to a Hetzner Storage Box and Pika backups to an external drive. Each setup did different things, and there was no documentation. Testing the backups was a random and incomplete process that was too much work so I never did it. Rsync is easy and fast, but using the --delete option means that if I accidentally delete something and the backup runs, it's gone. Basically, my backup &quot;system&quot; was riddled with gaps and potential problems. Rather than tweak that, I borrowed ideas from <a href="https://michaelharley.net/posts/2026/08/11/my-backup-strategy-2026/">My backup strategy 2026, by Michael Harley</a> and started over.</p>
<p>Like Mike, I went with <a href="https://www.borgbackup.org/">Borg</a> for backups and <a href="https://torsion.org/borgmatic/">borgmatic</a> for managing them.</p>
<p>borgmatic was new to me, so I decided to have Claude Code help with the configuration. This was a great idea, because Claude surfaced many things that could go wrong that I hadn't thought about.</p>
<p>The approach was to create separate install scripts that builds the borgmatic configs for me. They're idempotent, so if parts of them change, I can rerun without stepping on anything. I can read the scripts, so they're not magic, just better than what I'd have done on my own. The resulting borgmatic .yaml files are not complex, and are loaded with helpful comments.</p>
<p>I've only done this for my desktop Linux machine, but it's a great template for when I add the laptop and eventually the NAS.</p>
<p>Once everything was finished, Claude summarized our work.</p>
<p>Claude Code:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One timer, four config files, three shell scripts you can read. Mount guards on both drives, failure notifications, a dead-man's switch for the runs that never happen, rolling verification that never blocks the window, keys in pass and on a LUKS stick, the passphrase on paper off-site.</p>
<p>And, the part most setups skip, every claim tested rather than assumed. Contents verified per-directory against the live tree, not just totals. The unattended run watched end to end. The disaster path exercised with only a passphrase and an SSH key.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's all very &quot;belts and braces&quot;, but it's also the most complete personal backup setup I've ever had.</p>
